## Where the location comes from

`DefinitionStore::libraryFunction` (`src/runtime/definitions.cpp`) is the one place that turns a
pass module that did not build into a `problem` string. When the module's first error has a
position, it now builds a `DiagLabel` from it and caches it with the function entry:

* `firstErrorLocation()` copies the first error out of the module's own diagnostics, resolves a
  `TokenRange` with that module's parser (`TokenPositions`), and only accepts a `CharRange`. Its
  text is the diagnostic's own `name: message`; its path is `diagnosticPath()` of that diagnostic.
* `problem` becomes `its library '<module>' does not compile` — without the reason, because the
  reason is now the note.
* The label is stored in the `FunctionEntry`, so resolving the same pass a second time on the same
  context does not lose it.
* A failure whose first error has **no** position (an imported module failed, or there is no
  parser) keeps the old `its library '<module>' does not compile: <first error>` form, so nothing
  is lost. `library()` — the definitions path, a different caller — is untouched.

`registerCamelPass` (`src/execute/camel_pass.h`) passes an optional `DiagLabel` out to
`resolvePassWithModule`, `ResolvedPass` carries it next to `problem`, and `invalidParameterDiagnostic()`
pushes it into `diag.related`; `requireKnownPasses()` hands it over. That is the same `related`
field the compiler's own secondary locations use, so all four formats and the language server
carry it without new code.

The rule is "the first error if it has a position", not "only `SyntaxDiag`": a semantic error in a
pass module is attached the same way. The syntax error is the case the review asked about and the
only one a test pins.

